# {{ ctx.title }}

An opening of 2 or 3 plain sentences: what this part of the system does, what it takes in and hands on, and why the rest of the system needs it. No file paths, numbers or identifiers in the opening. Do not open with a frame: "The X layer is the Y of Z", "X serves as the ...", "X acts as the ...", "X is the entry stage of ...", "it consumes ... and produces ..." and "This module contains N files" are all banned, because two pages that open with the same first five words read as one.

Then one diagram in a fenced `mermaid` block, of the type the facts' `diagram` field names:
- `sequenceDiagram`: draw `traced_flow` as one operation, with 4 to 6 participants and 6 to 10 messages. Participants are the parts involved under plain names, declared with one-word ids (`participant Store as Result store`); an outside caller or callee may be one participant. Messages follow the traced calls in order, each written as a short plain-English phrase for what the call does ("resolve call targets"), never the function's name.
- `flowchart`: `flowchart LR` with 4 to 9 nodes for the parts that appear in `imports_between_parts`, under plain names, the directory in small text on a second line (`Resolvers["Resolvers<br/><small>resolvers/</small>"]`). Draw at most 10 edges, each one taken from `imports_between_parts`, pointing the way data or control flows and labelled with a short verb phrase (`-->|resolves imports for|`). At most 2 outside nodes from `depends_on` or `used_by`, marked `:::ext`, with `classDef ext stroke-dasharray:4 3;`.
- `none`: no diagram.
Node and participant ids are short CamelCase words (`GraphViews`), never a mermaid keyword such as `end`, `graph` or `style`. `classDef`, `class` and `style` lines belong in flowcharts only.

